five factors to selling ad space and  setting prices so I got a great question  for my YouTube viewers love you guys  able Abe studios who wrote in to say  great video Evans it seems like every  question I search for an answer for one  of your videos pops up very cool my  question now is if you we're developing  an art photography magazine how would  you go about pricing ad spaces would it  be the size of the ad the frequency the  creative development or something else  go so able Abe and follow you people out  there one in the certain magazine here  are the five factors you need to look at  when you're setting your ad prices and  trying to sell them  number one is circulation this is going  to be the first question everybody asks  you how many people read your magazine  right if it's a national magazine and  you're selling a full-page ad that could  be a quarter of a million dollars if  you're much smaller magazine that could  be ten thousand dollars or two thousand  dollars you got to know how many people  are actually reading your magazine and  the advertisers again it seemed that the  first thing they asked you for number  two is demographics who is actually  reading the magazine who is your  audience and the more specific and niche  and defined it is the easier it is to  charge more money so for example if you  had a magazine that was trying to reach  really hard to access people say it was  CEOs of Fortune 500 companies that's who  was reading the magazine while your  circulation is getting really small  right there's only 500 people maximum  who could read it because it's a fortune  500 but that's a really targeted select  group of people that people would pay  big money advertising would really want  to get in there and spend their cash to  reach those people so the more clearly  defined your demographics are the more  you can say with the age the sex the the  interests and hobbies and and more  demographic information the more you can  throw out there the better it is and the  harder those people are to reach the  more you can increase your prices number  three is the size of the ad so how much  ad space are we talking about usually go  by percentage of the page so if it's a  full page maybe that's $10,000 if it's a  half page maybe that's $6,000 right the  more space that they want the more you  charge for it number four is frequency  how often is your magazine coming out  in how many editions is the advertiser  going to be advertising in it for so for  example if your magazine comes out once  a month are they just buying in for that  once you know they only buy for one ad  spot in that first edition and that's it  or are they going to be doing it every  month for six months or 12 months right  if it's a one-off deal then you might  sell an ad space for $2,000 but if it's  a commitment to do this for six months  and you could sell that same ad space  for discounted price say $1,500 because  it committed to you for a number of  months and you don't have to go out and  sell that spot to somebody else and  number five is positioning where do they  get the app most advertisers want to be  either on the back cover on the inside  of the front cover or as close to the  front as possible and the rates can go  up the closer to the front so if I'm  getting a an inside page on a national  magazine that could cost me half a  million dollars but that same page for  the Rinne will cost a lot less so you  can sell your rates depending on where  they are in the positioning other  magazine so the top five things and look  at our circulation demographics size  frequency and positioning now the  magazine industry is super traditional a  kind of boring honestly and it's easy to  find out what the rates are of your  competition so honestly what I look at  is try to find somebody who has a  similar magazine similar demographic  similar size and see what they're  charging you know you can get those  rates and charge something similar right